{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, truncate observed histories to the most recent 64 positions instead of 128, reserving the final position for the appended mask at inference. Retain chronological nonpadding position ranks and the masked-position readout. Most histories fit within 64 positions; removing distant context may reduce interference. Keep positional-table shape, embeddings, backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed, and apply the same history policy in training and the predictor writer.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, introduce a fixed training-frequency input cue: for each visible real item, scale its existing input vector by 1 + 0.05*tanh((mean_log_count - log1p(count))/std_log_count), with statistics computed over real-item training counts and standard deviation clamped above zero. Keep padding zero and mask scale one. This mildly emphasizes rarer context without the previous history-relative normalization. Preserve embedding parameterization, topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer; checkpoint the feature statistics for prediction.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, use label smoothing of 0.02 in the existing full-catalog masked-item cross-entropy, retaining equal weighting across masked positions and temperature 1. Mild smoothing may reduce overconfidence on sparse histories while preserving target discrimination. Change only the loss; retain input features, embeddings, topology, scoring, readout, masking and negative sampling, optimizer, scheduler, batch settings and epoch limits.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, untie real-item input and output embedding tables while preserving width 64 and the current row normalization and radius. Initialize the output table by copying the initial real-item input rows, then train both jointly within the same BERT4Rec network. Separate context and prediction representations may ease conflicting gradients without the radius increase that previously regressed. Keep dot-product scoring, history, topology, loss and optimizer fixed; update checkpoint loading and the predictor writer consistently."
}
